<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Personal Success Story – Lesson # 2</h2>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Personal Success Story</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Am Successful</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze opinion editorials on general interest topics. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze opinion pieces on personal success, identifying main arguments (e.g., "If young adults start planning their finances early, they will build a strong foundation for future success.")</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can compare persuasive language in articles on financial and career planning, identifying different viewpoints (e.g., "One article argues that savings should be prioritized, while another suggests investing small amounts monthly for long-term growth.")</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can discuss editorial viewpoints on success strategies (e.g., "The author uses personal stories, such as how he/she managed his/her finances carefully and saved for future goals, to promote responsible spending.")</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images on the board or projector, showing various aspects of success (e.g., a person graduating, someone managing finances, a volunteer group, a person working on a project). Students are asked to quickly read a short, impactful quote related to "success" (e.g., "Success is not final, failure is not fatal: it is the courage to continue that counts."). In pairs, they briefly discuss which image resonates most with the quote and why, using vocabulary from Lesson 1 like <i>goals, achievements, initiative</i>.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express personal connections to the images and quote. For example, pointing to an image of someone volunteering, the teacher says: "This image shows <i>community service</i>. I have volunteered for years, and it has given me great personal <i>growth</i>. I believe this aligns with 'the courage to continue.'" The teacher explicitly stresses content words like "community SERVICE", "perSONAL GROWTH", and "courage to conTINue", reducing stress on auxiliary verbs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the theme and key terms.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main idea of our warm-up activity? Is it about failure or about different ways to achieve success?" (Expected answer: "Different ways to achieve success.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I said 'community SERVICE' with stress on 'service,' what kind of word is 'service'?" (Expected answer: "A noun / a content word.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Personal Success Story")</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides a B1.3 level opinion editorial titled "Investing in Your Future: Beyond Just Savings" (approximately 300-350 words). The article discusses various strategies for young adults to achieve success, including financial planning, skill development, and community involvement. It uses vocabulary such as <i>finances, investment, career, growth, opportunities, strategies, contribute, manage, aspire, long-term, future-oriented</i>. It also naturally incorporates present perfect (e.g., "I have always believed in..."), future continuous (e.g., "In five years, I will be pursuing..."), and first conditional (e.g., "If you invest early, you will see significant growth."). The article presents different viewpoints on prioritizing savings vs. investing in oneself.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze opinion editorials on general interest topics. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article individually for 2 minutes to grasp the main idea. Then, they choose the best summary statement from three options provided by the teacher (e.g., a) The article discusses how to save money quickly, b) The article explains various strategies for young adults to achieve long-term success, c) The article is about finding a job immediately after high school).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article again to determine if five statements are True or False, correcting the false ones by citing specific sentences from the text. (e.g., "According to the article, focusing only on immediate savings guarantees future success." - False, the article suggests investing in skills too.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key arguments from the article with the specific strategies they describe (e.g., Match 'Building a strong network' with 'Collaborating with professionals can broaden your opportunities').</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Analyzing opinion pieces for main arguments, comparing persuasive language, identifying different viewpoints. Grammar: Present perfect, Future continuous, First conditional. Vocabulary: <i>success, finances, savings, investment, career, growth, opportunities, strategies, achieve, manage, invest, professional, essential, long-term, future-oriented</i>.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences extracted from the editorial, containing gaps where target vocabulary or grammatical structures (Present Perfect, Future Continuous, First Conditional) should be. They fill in the blanks using words from a provided box or by recalling from the article. For instance: "If young adults ______ (continue) their studies, they _______ (find) more <i>opportunities</i>." (Answer: If young adults <i>continue</i> their studies, they <i>will find</i> more opportunities.) or "She ______ (manage) her <i>finances</i> carefully for years, so she ______ (build) a strong foundation." (Answer: She <i>has managed</i> her <i>finances</i> carefully for years, so she <i>has built</i> a strong foundation.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Argument Identification):</b> In pairs, students re-read paragraphs of the editorial. Their task is to identify and underline sentences that express: a) a main argument, b) a persuasive statement, and c) a different viewpoint. They then categorize these sentences. For example, they might underline "Early financial planning is <i>essential</i> for future <i>success</i>" as a main argument and "One article argues that <i>savings</i> should be prioritized, while another suggests <i>investing</i> small amounts monthly for <i>long-term growth</i>" as identifying different viewpoints.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Evidence Hunt):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair is assigned one of the following topics: "Financial Planning," "Career Development," or "Community Involvement." They must re-read the editorial to find at least three sentences (evidence) that support the author's viewpoint on their assigned topic. They write these sentences down and highlight the target vocabulary and grammar structures used. For example, for "Financial Planning," a student might write: "The author states, 'I <i>have saved</i> some money for my college studies and <i>have invested</i> in several online courses to broaden my skills,' demonstrating careful <i>finances</i> and <i>investment</i>." They practice respectfully discussing personal achievements and goals while referencing the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Personal Success Story")</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary & Viewpoint Discussion.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students form small groups (3-4 students). Each group is given a specific prompt related to the editorial. For example:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Summarize the main arguments for both financial savings and investing in skills presented in the editorial. Then, discuss which viewpoint you find more persuasive and why, using examples from your own experiences or aspirations."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Analyze how the author uses personal stories (e.g., managing finances carefully, volunteering) to promote their success strategies. Identify at least two such instances and discuss their impact on the reader."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups must collaboratively read and discuss, then appoint a speaker to present their analysis and conclusion to the class, using the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>strategies, initiative, achievements, future-oriented</i>) and grammar (e.g., "The author <i>has argued</i> that...", "If we follow this advice, we <i>will be building</i> a solid future..."). They should ensure all voices are heard and respected, integrating sociolinguistic competence.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present their analysis and discussion from the Production stage.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students clearly identified main arguments from the editorial.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students accurately compared different viewpoints and persuasive language.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students used target vocabulary (e.g., <i>finances, investment, opportunities, strategies</i>) appropriately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students correctly applied Present Perfect, Future Continuous, and First Conditional structures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation demonstrated good stress on content words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group discussion involved respectful engagement and diverse voices.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new strategy for personal success I learned from today's reading is: _______________."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now better analyze an opinion piece by (circle one): finding the main arguments / comparing different viewpoints / discussing the author's strategies."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy helped me the most today to understand the editorial's message and different viewpoints?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the ideas from this article on 'How I Am Successful' apply to my own future goals and aspirations?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The comprehension tasks and group discussion activities worked well for students to analyze the editorial. Students were able to identify main arguments and different viewpoints. Some students needed more scaffolding to compare persuasive language, possibly requiring more explicit examples of persuasive techniques. Adjustments for the next lesson should include ensuring a clear link between identified arguments and their persuasive impact. For pronunciation, I will continue to emphasize content word stress when providing feedback on their spoken analysis.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The analytical skills developed in this reading lesson will be crucial for the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), where students will actively discuss and debate their personal success strategies and future goals based on the ideas read today, engaging in more direct oral interaction.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>